# November 28, 2026: historic state hospitals and asylums from New Jersey to West Virginia, a batch of the themed pass on
# asylums. Sixteen institutions: three in New Jersey, one in Delaware, five in Maryland, five in Virginia and two in West
# Virginia. Already on the map and left alone: the Salem County Insane Asylum, the Trans-Allegheny Lunatic Asylum at
# Weston and St. Elizabeths in Washington.
# Sources opened in this session: Wikipedia's articles on each institution and its list of Kirkbride Plan hospitals; the
# Park Service's National Register records and forms (Sheppard Pratt, the Warfield Complex at Springfield, the Western
# State Hospital Complex, the Henderson Building, the Central State Hospital Chapel, the Greystone gas plant); SAH
# Archipedia (Delaware State Hospital, Spencer, Western State, the Henderson Building); the Virginia Department of
# Historic Resources; Spring Grove's own history page; Anne Arundel County's Crownsville page; the Town of Sykesville's
# Warfield page; Colonial Williamsburg; Clio; e-WV, the West Virginia Encyclopedia; HMdb markers; Cardinal News; the
# Stevenson Villager; the New Jersey Historic Trust; Preservation New Jersey; Patch; and Community News.
# No patient is named. Pins: Wikipedia, Register, geocoder and OpenStreetMap points, each looked at on the aerial.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
BATCH={'key':'asylum_midatl_ccvw','label':'State hospitals and asylums, New Jersey to West Virginia','added':'2026-11-28 07:25'}
KB=W('Kirkbride Plan')
def NR(n,t): return ['National Register record: '+t+' ('+n+')','https://npgallery.nps.gov/AssetDetail/NRIS/'+n]
PLACES=[
N('trenton-state-hospital-nj','Trenton State Hospital (New Jersey State Lunatic Asylum)','Ewing Township','njc','inst','standing',40.2436,-74.8055,'site','view','drive','','1848–',
  "Dorothea Dix's testimony to the New Jersey legislature led to the state's first public mental hospital, which opened on May 15, 1848, as the New Jersey State Lunatic Asylum. Wikipedia calls it the first hospital designed on Thomas Kirkbride's plan, with John Notman as the architect. Dix herself had rooms there from 1881 and died there in 1887. From 1907 to 1930 its medical director, Henry Cotton, had patients' teeth and other organs taken out in the belief that infection caused madness, with hundreds of deaths; the pulling of teeth went on until 1960. It is still the state's Trenton Psychiatric Hospital.",
  'Community News says the first Kirkbride asylum still stands, but no source read names which of the buildings on the campus is the 1848 one, so the pin is on the campus and not on a building.',
  '','An active state psychiatric hospital in the south of Ewing Township, in the area once called Asylum Station. Most buildings sit back behind woods and lawns from Sullivan Way; along Stuyvesant Avenue there are high fences, barbed wire and guard posts. View from the public road only.',
  [W('Trenton Psychiatric Hospital'),KB,['Community News: The asylum at Asylum Station','https://communitynews.org/?p=305808']],brick='?',conf='mixed',
  arch='John Notman',archw="Wikipedia's article on the hospital: \"The architect was the Scottish-American John Notman\""),
N('greystone-park-state-hospital-site-nj','Greystone Park State Hospital site (State Asylum for the Insane at Morris Plains)','Parsippany-Troy Hills','njn','inst','gone',40.83472,-74.50528,'site','view','drive','','1876–2015',
  "New Jersey's second state asylum, conceived in 1871 to relieve the crowded one at Trenton, opened in 1876. Samuel Sloan designed the main building on the Kirkbride plan: a centre for the offices and a wing for each sex, 673,700 square feet, built for a few hundred patients and holding more than 7,700 at its peak. The last patients moved in 2008 and 2009 to a new state hospital on the hill above, and the main building, whose footprint was said to be second only to the Pentagon's, was pulled down between April and October 2015 despite protests. The state wanted its part of the ground kept as open space; Morris County had bought about 300 acres in 2001 for a dollar and made Central Park of Morris County of it, after clearing the smaller buildings there.",
  "Nothing of the main building stands. The hospital's gas works, the Illumination Gas Plant on Old Dover Road, designed by Sloan and William Farmer, was put on the National Register in 2000; whether it still stands was not checked. The new Greystone hospital is on the hill above the site.",
  '','The site of the main building is state land; Morris County\'s Central Park next to it has ball fields, a playground and trails. The new state hospital above it is an active facility. Whether the old building\'s site is open to walk on was not checked: look from the public roads.',
  [W('Greystone Park Psychiatric Hospital'),KB,['New Jersey Historic Trust: Greystone','https://www.nj.gov/dca/njht/funded/sitedetails/greystone.shtml'],['Preservation New Jersey: Greystone','https://preservationnj.org/10-most/greystone-park-state-psychiatric-hospital/'],['Morristown Green: Greystone is vanishing (2015)','https://morristowngreen.com/2015/07/08/stone-by-stone-pillar-by-pillar-greystone-is-vanishing/'],['Patch: Kirkbride\'s final chapter (2014)','https://patch.com/new-jersey/morris/kirkbrides-final-chapter'],NR('00000653','Illumination Gas Plant of the New Jersey State Asylum for the Insane at Morris Plains')],brick='?',
  arch='Samuel Sloan',archw="Wikipedia's article: \"Samuel Sloan was named architect of the main building and its smaller supporting buildings\"; the New Jersey Historic Trust names Sloan too"),
N('skillman-village-nj','New Jersey State Village for Epileptics (Skillman Village), now Skillman Park','Skillman','njc','inst','gone',40.415,-74.691,'site','public','walk','','1898–1998',
  "A law signed by Governor Foster M. Voorhees in 1898 set up the State Village for Epileptics, so that people with epilepsy need not be sent to insane asylums. The state put it together from six farms bought between 1889 and 1920, and it was meant to stand on its own: schools, a theatre, a working farm, a firehouse, water works, a landfill, housing and a power plant. Short of money and staff through the Depression and the war, it was nicknamed \"the Snake Pit of New Jersey\"; later called the North Princeton Developmental Center, it closed in 1995 and the last residents left in 1998. Montgomery Township took it over, sold 247 acres to Somerset County in 2011, and nearly everything was pulled down by 2012.",
  'Skillman Park, opened in 2015, keeps much of the tree-lined layout the landscape architect Charles Leavitt drew in 1901, with a paved loop trail of about 2.2 miles and a path along Rock Brook. Wikipedia says one or two of the original buildings still stand; the Smalley Theater of 1916 was pulled down and the house called Maplewood burned.',
  '','A Somerset County park, with several parking lots and a road through it. Park hours were not checked.',
  [W('North Princeton Developmental Center'),W('Skillman Park'),['New Jersey Historic Trust: Skillman Village','https://www.nj.gov/dca/njht/funded/sitedetails/skillman_village.shtml']],brick='?',th=[40.41427,-74.69412],thn='Skillman Park, Parking Lot B'),
N('delaware-state-hospital-farnhurst-de','Delaware State Hospital (Delaware Hospital for the Insane), Farnhurst','Farnhurst','de','inst','standing',39.70073,-75.57684,'site','view','drive','','1894–',
  "The state's asylum grew up beside the Delaware Almshouse, which had moved out of Wilmington to the Blandy Farm at Hare's Corner in the 1880s. The Main Building of 1894 and 1895 was designed by Elijah James Dallett of Baker and Dallett, with women downstairs and men upstairs; an Annex behind it, built in 1897 for $35,000 to get patients out of the basement, held more than 200 by 1939 and became the first receiving ward in 1949. Brown and Whiteside added two H-shaped Art Deco buildings in 1930 to 1932, and a chapel built by the Works Progress Administration opened on September 28, 1939. The almshouse building, a red brick Italianate design of 1882 to 1884 by Stephen Decatur Button, was pulled down in 1967. The hospital is now the Delaware Psychiatric Center, the state's only public psychiatric hospital for adults.",
  'The Main Building with many later additions, the Annex behind it (now offices), the Debnam Building (the 1930s Continued Treatment Building, in use) and the empty Re-Educational Building stand, says SAH Archipedia. The campus has a walking history tour on Clio, and a spiral cemetery and a community garden.',
  '','The Herman M. Holloway Sr. Campus of the Delaware Department of Health and Social Services, 1901 North Dupont Highway, near US 13 and I-295. An active state hospital and office campus: view from the public way.',
  [['SAH Archipedia: Herman M. Holloway Sr. Campus (Delaware State Hospital)','https://sah-archipedia.org/node/10459'],['Clio: the Annex','https://theclio.com/entry/174464'],['Clio: Delaware Psychiatric Center','https://theclio.com/entry/174468'],['Clio: the Chapel','https://theclio.com/entry/174355']],brick='?',
  arch='Baker and Dallett; Brown and Whiteside',archw='SAH Archipedia: the Main Building was "designed by Elijah James Dallett for Baker and Dallett"; the Continued Treatment and Re-Educational buildings of 1930 to 1932 by Brown and Whiteside'),
N('spring-grove-state-hospital-md','Spring Grove State Hospital (Maryland Hospital for the Insane)','Catonsville','md','inst','gone',39.26472,-76.7225,'area','view','drive','','1853–',
  "Founded in Baltimore in 1797 as the Maryland Hospital, it is counted the second-oldest psychiatric hospital in the country after Eastern State in Williamsburg. In 1852 the state bought 136 acres near Catonsville, and J. Crawford Neilson designed a Kirkbride-plan Main Building in the Italianate style for 250 patients, begun in 1853 and slowed by the Civil War; the hospital moved there on October 7, 1872. It was renamed Spring Grove State Hospital in 1912. The Main Building was pulled down in 1964, says the hospital's own history, or in 1963, says Wikipedia, and replaced by modern buildings. It is still a state psychiatric hospital, and its land was handed to the University of Maryland, Baltimore County in May 2022.",
  "The hospital's history names buildings from the early years that still stand: the laundry (first the Main Building's powerhouse and gas works), the original firehouse, a locksmith building and other outbuildings, and the boiler house just west of where the Main Building stood.",
  '','An active state hospital on Wade Avenue, Catonsville: view from the public road only.',
  [W('Spring Grove Hospital Center'),['Spring Grove Hospital Center: history','https://health.maryland.gov/springgrove/phototours/Pages/history.aspx'],KB],brick='?',conf='mixed',
  arch='J. Crawford Neilson',archw="Spring Grove Hospital Center's history: Neilson designed the Main Building and oversaw its building from 1853 to 1872"),
N('springfield-state-hospital-warfield-md','Springfield State Hospital, Warfield Complex (Second Hospital for the Insane of Maryland)','Sykesville','md','inst','standing',39.37861,-76.96139,'site','view','drive','','1896–',
  "The state bought the Springfield estate from Governor Frank Brown and opened the Second Hospital for the Insane of Maryland in 1896, built on the cottage plan of separate buildings rather than one great Kirkbride building; Wikipedia calls it the second in the country to use it, after Medfield. Joseph Evans Sperry of Baltimore designed the women's group in 1898, Colonial Revival buildings joined by porches round a service building, and it grew to 16 buildings by 1939, among them the Hubner Building and the T Building of 1938 by Henry Powell Hopkins. The hospital still runs on a smaller scale elsewhere on the grounds. The women's group, on the National Register since 2000 as the Warfield Complex, Hubner and T Buildings, was used by the hospital until the early 2000s.",
  'Red brick laid in Flemish bond, with limestone, marble and slate, says the Register form. The Town of Sykesville counts twelve historic buildings on a 17-acre parcel, now Warfield at Historic Sykesville: three restored and let to businesses, nine waiting for new users, with a town park of 27 acres planned.',
  '','On Maryland Route 32 in Sykesville. A private mixed-use development, with offices in the restored buildings: look from the public roads. The rest of Springfield is an active state hospital.',
  [W('Springfield Hospital Center'),NR('00001271','Warfield Complex, Hubner, and T Buildings'),['Town of Sykesville: Warfield at Historic Sykesville','https://www.townofsykesville.gov/2187/Warfield-at-Historic-Sykesville']],brick='yes',bcol='red',
  arch='Joseph Evans Sperry; Henry Powell Hopkins',archw='National Register form: "Noted Baltimore architect Joseph Evans Sperry designed the group in 1898"; "The T Building was designed by Henry Powell Hopkins and constructed in 1938"'),
N('crownsville-state-hospital-md','Crownsville State Hospital (Hospital for the Negro Insane of Maryland)','Crownsville','md','inst','standing',39.02383,-76.60118,'site','view','drive','','1911–2004',
  "An act of April 11, 1910 set up the Hospital for the Negro Insane of Maryland, outside Baltimore; the state bought old willow and tobacco farmland at Crownsville that December for $19,000. The first twelve patients came on March 13, 1911 and lived at first in a willow-curing house, and patients were set to work building the hospital, beginning with A Building in October 1912. Crowding and tuberculosis marked its early decades; the first African American on its staff was hired in 1948, and George McKenzie Phillips later became its first African American superintendent. It closed in July 2004, and Anne Arundel County took it over from the state in December 2022 as Crownsville Hospital Memorial Park.",
  "Wikipedia says the original buildings still stand. The county counts 76 structures on about 500 acres; the core historic group of ten buildings, with about 20 acres round it, is under a Maryland Historical Trust easement. The patients' cemetery has a memorial, \"Say My Name\".",
  '','Anne Arundel County land, being planned as a campus for nonprofits (a food bank and others are already there). The county\'s page sends visitors to the cemetery through its Bacon Ridge Natural Area page; it gives no hours for the old buildings. Look from the public roads.',
  [W('Crownsville Hospital Center'),['Anne Arundel County: Crownsville Hospital Memorial Park','https://www.aacounty.org/CrownsvillePark']],brick='?'),
N('sheppard-pratt-hospital-md','Sheppard and Enoch Pratt Hospital (Sheppard Asylum) and Gatehouse','Towson','md','inst','standing',39.391111,-76.619167,'site','view','drive','','1860–',
  "The Baltimore merchant Moses Sheppard, moved by Dorothea Dix, left his fortune for an asylum, founded in 1853 on the Mount Airy Farm bought in 1858. The gatehouse on Charles Street was built first, in May 1860, to the design of Thomas and James M. Dixon. Calvert Vaux designed the hospital itself on the Kirkbride plan, and its cornerstone was laid in 1862, but money ran short and it did not open until 1891, renamed for a second benefactor, Enoch Pratt. It is a private psychiatric hospital still, and a National Historic Landmark since 1971; a large expansion in the 2000s moved patients out of the old buildings into new ones.",
  "Vaux's two brick buildings, one for men and one for women, mirror each other: three and a half storeys, each 360 feet long with a six-storey tower, cross gables and oriel windows, a mix of English Norman and Italianate, says the Register form, which does not give the brick's colour. A modern central building of 1971 fills the courtyard between them. The gatehouse is Tudor Revival in field stone, with a central archway for carriages, and was built by John L. Gittier.",
  '','A private hospital on North Charles Street, Towson. Look from the street.',
  [W('Sheppard and Enoch Pratt Hospital'),NR('71000369','Sheppard and Enoch Pratt Hospital and Gatehouse'),KB],brick='?',
  arch='Calvert Vaux; Thomas and James M. Dixon',builder='John L. Gittier',archw='National Register record and form: the hospital by Calvert Vaux; the Gatehouse "designed by Thomas and James M. Dixon of Baltimore in 1860 and constructed by John L. Gittier, builder"'),
N('rosewood-training-school-md','Rosewood State Training School (Asylum and Training School for the Feeble-Minded)','Owings Mills','md','inst','standing',39.42312,-76.77113,'area','view','drive','','1888–2009',
  "Maryland's legislature founded the Asylum and Training School for the Feeble-Minded in 1888, with only $10,000 for land and buildings. It was the Rosewood State Training School from 1912 to 1961, then Rosewood State Hospital and from 1969 the Rosewood Center. The state announced its closing in January 2008 and it shut on June 30, 2009; a vacant building burned that March. In 2017 the state sold 117 of its 276 acres to Stevenson University for a dollar, and 18 buildings there were pulled down from that November for playing fields, which opened in 2022.",
  "The Stevenson Villager wrote in 2017 that the state kept its remaining historic buildings, set off from the new fields by a band of trees, and the aerial shows several old buildings still standing round the pin; their condition was not checked.",
  '','Stevenson University\'s athletic fields and the state\'s land on Rosewood Lane. The old buildings are state property and closed: look from the public road.',
  [W('Rosewood Center'),['Stevenson Villager: Rosewood (2017)','https://stevensonvillager.com/?p=13089']],brick='?',conf='mixed'),
N('public-hospital-1773-williamsburg-va','Public Hospital of 1773 (Eastern State Hospital), reconstructed','Williamsburg','va','inst','standing',37.268912,-76.704738,'exact','open','walk','','1773–',
  "Governor Francis Fauquier asked the House of Burgesses in 1766 to provide for people \"deprived of their senses\", and the Public Hospital admitted its first patient on October 12, 1773: the first public building in what is now the United States built only for the care of the mentally ill. As Eastern State Hospital it grew through the 1800s; the original building burned on June 7, 1885, from newly added electric wiring. Between 1937 and 1968 the patients were moved to a new hospital outside town, where Eastern State still runs. The Colonial Williamsburg Foundation rebuilt the 1773 building on its excavated foundations in 1985.",
  'A reconstruction, not the original building. For years it was the way into the art museums behind it, through an underground passage.',
  '','On Francis Street in the Historic Area, run by Colonial Williamsburg. Its page gave the hours below, and also said it was closed at the time; check before going.',
  [W('Eastern State Hospital (Virginia)'),['Colonial Williamsburg: Public Hospital','https://www.colonialwilliamsburg.org/locations/public-hospital/'],['WYDaily: new art museums entrance (2014)','https://wydaily.com/latest/local/2014/12/01/with-new-entrance-cw-hopes-to-make-art-museums-more-visible-to-guests/']],brick='?',hours='Thu 1:30–3 p.m.'),
N('western-state-hospital-staunton-va','Western State Hospital (Western Lunatic Asylum), now the Blackburn Inn','Staunton','va','inst','standing',38.14512,-79.06717,'site','open','walk','','1825–',
  "Founded in 1825 as the Western Lunatic Asylum, it took its first patient on July 24, 1828, in a five-part main building designed by William Small of Baltimore. Robert Cary Long Jr. drew the annexes of the early 1840s, and Thomas R. Blackburn, a protégé of Thomas Jefferson, gave it its classical porticoes: the hotel's history puts his work in the mid-1830s, while the state historic office dates the porticoes to 1847 and his refectory and chapel to 1851. Renamed Western State Hospital in 1894, it moved to new buildings in the 1970s, and the old ones became a state prison until 2002 or 2003 (the sources differ). Sold in 2005, they became condominiums and, in 2018, the Blackburn Inn.",
  'A Greek Revival main building with porticoes, and 22 contributing buildings in the National Register district, among them the U-shaped North Ward with its giant pilasters and octagonal cupola, a steam plant and a dairy barn.',
  '','301 Greenville Avenue. The main building is a working hotel, the Blackburn Inn; the rest of the complex holds homes and businesses.',
  [W('Western State Hospital (Staunton, Virginia)'),NR('69000362','Western State Hospital Complex'),['Virginia Department of Historic Resources: Western State Hospital Complex','https://dhr.virginia.gov/?p=15463'],['SAH Archipedia: Western State Hospital','https://sah-archipedia.org/node/1617'],['Historic Hotels of America: Blackburn Inn history','https://www.historichotels.org/us/hotels-resorts/blackburn-inn/history']],brick='?',hours='?',conf='mixed',
  arch='William Small; Robert Cary Long Jr.; Thomas R. Blackburn',builder='William B. Phillips',archw='Virginia Department of Historic Resources: William Small designed the 1828 main building, Robert Cary Long Jr. the annexes with William B. Phillips as contractor, Thomas Blackburn built the 1851 refectory and chapel; the Blackburn Inn\'s history names Thomas R. Blackburn for the 1830s work'),
N('central-state-hospital-petersburg-va','Central State Hospital (Central Lunatic Asylum) and its unmarked cemetery','Petersburg','va','inst','traces',37.20093,-77.45460,'site','view','drive','','1870–',
  "At the end of 1869 the Confederate hospital at Howard's Grove was given over to Black Virginians with mental illness, and in 1870 the state incorporated it as the Central Lunatic Asylum, the first such institution for Black patients in the country. Petersburg bought the Mayfield Farm in 1882 and gave it to the state, and on March 22, 1885, 373 patients moved into a new red brick hospital trimmed with grey granite, a four-storey centre with wings on the Kirkbride plan. That building has been pulled down (Wikipedia thinks in the late 1960s or early 1970s), and the brick chapel of 1904, on the National Register from 2010, fell in May 2014 after years empty. The hospital took only Black patients until the mid-1960s and is still a state hospital.",
  "The unmarked cemetery, about four acres, was used from 1884 to 1924; ground radar suggests 4,200 to 4,500 graves, and the state has put up signs and a small parking area.",
  '','The cemetery is at Seventh Avenue and Westmoreland Circle on the hospital grounds; the hospital, at 26317 West Washington Street, is an active state facility. Whether the cemetery is open to walk in was not confirmed: view from the road.',
  [W('Central State Hospital (Virginia)'),W('Central State Hospital Chapel'),['Virginia Department of Historic Resources: Central State Hospital Chapel','https://www.dhr.virginia.gov/historic-registers/026-0123-0005/'],['Virginia Department of Historic Resources: the unmarked cemetery','https://dhr.virginia.gov/?p=24463'],['Central State Hospital: our history','https://dbhds.virginia.gov/facilities/csh/our-history/']],brick='?'),
N('southwestern-state-hospital-marion-va','Southwestern State Hospital, Henderson Building','Marion','va','inst','standing',36.83331,-81.51203,'exact','view','drive','','1887–',
  "Southwestern State Hospital opened in 1887 to serve the white population of southwest Virginia; its first Black patient was admitted in 1967. The administration building, built in 1887 to a design by the McDonald Brothers of Louisville, was four storeys with a 118-foot clock tower, and three ward wings radiated from it on the Kirkbride plan. In 1930 and 1931 it was remade in the Classical Revival style: the tower came off for a domed rotunda, a storey came off, and a two-storey loggia went on, and it was named for E. H. Henderson, superintendent from 1915 to 1927. The wings were replaced in the 1980s. On the National Register since 1990; the hospital is now the Southwestern Virginia Mental Health Institute.",
  'Dark brick with limestone lintels, says SAH Archipedia, and a loggia of brick and Indiana limestone with Doric columns below and Ionic arches above. Nearby is the Tubercular Building of 1937 to 1939, by Eubank and Caldwell, on the Register since 2023.',
  '','340 Bagley Circle, on East Main Street. An active state hospital: view from the public road only.',
  [W('Henderson Building'),NR('89001919','Henderson Building, Southwestern State Hospital'),['SAH Archipedia: Henderson Building','https://sah-archipedia.org/node/49129'],['Virginia Department of Historic Resources: Tubercular Building','https://www.dhr.virginia.gov/historic-registers/119-5006-0004/']],brick='no',bcol='dark',
  arch='McDonald Brothers; Eubank and Caldwell',builder='Lewman and Sweeny',archw='SAH Archipedia: designed by McDonald Brothers of Louisville, built by Lewman and Sweeny of Columbus, Indiana, the 1931 loggia by Eubank and Caldwell (the Park Service record spells them MacDonald Bros. and Lewman & Sweeney)'),
N('virginia-state-colony-madison-heights-va','Virginia State Colony for Epileptics and Feeble-Minded (Central Virginia Training Center)','Madison Heights','va','inst','standing',37.41583,-79.11972,'area','view','drive','','1910–2020',
  "A law of 1906, written by the eugenicist Aubrey Strode with Albert Priddy and Joseph DeJarnette, set up a colony to keep people with epilepsy and mental disabilities apart from society. It opened in 1910 beside the James River across from Lynchburg, first for men with epilepsy, and was at the centre of the Supreme Court case that upheld Virginia's sterilization law. It was renamed the Lynchburg State Colony in 1940, the Lynchburg Training School and Hospital in 1954 and the Central Virginia Training Center in 1983, and closed on April 2, 2020.",
  'About 350 acres with 95 empty buildings of many dates, and cemeteries. In 2026 the state planned to pull down 31 of the buildings by the autumn of 2027 and sell the land.',
  '','State land, guarded and empty, reached only by a road through a neighbourhood. Look from the public road.',
  [W('Virginia State Colony for Epileptics and Feebleminded'),['Cardinal News: the training center site (2026)','https://cardinalnews.org/2026/05/20/6-years-after-closing-amhersts-former-state-run-training-center-is-still-on-long-path-to-redevelopment/']],brick='?'),
N('spencer-state-hospital-wv','Spencer State Hospital site (Second Hospital for the Insane)','Spencer','wv','inst','gone',38.79776,-81.35039,'area','view','drive','','1893–1989',
  "Authorized in 1887 to relieve the hospital at Weston, it opened on July 18, 1893, on 184 acres that the Roane County Court bought in 1888 (e-WV says the county gave them). Franzheim, Giesey and Faris of Wheeling and William Howe Patton of Parkersburg designed it on the Kirkbride plan: connected Gothic buildings of red brick, 940 feet long with 358 rooms by 1904, said to be the longest continuous brick building in America. The administration building was replaced between 1973 and 1976; the hospital closed in June 1989 and its equipment was sold in 1993. The old buildings are gone: SAH Archipedia says overgrown rubble marks the site, and Clio says a Walmart stands on the grounds.",
  "A marker of 1997 at Spencer Elementary School tells of the hospital's cemeteries, one with some 750 unmarked graves, disturbed when the school was built.",
  '','The pin is at the Walmart that Clio puts on the grounds, a private store; where the rubble lies was not confirmed. The cemetery marker is at the elementary school.',
  [['SAH Archipedia: Spencer State Hospital','https://sah-archipedia.org/node/7070'],['Clio: Spencer State Hospital','https://www.theclio.com/entry/22871'],['e-WV: Spencer State Hospital','https://wvencyclopedia.org/entries/501'],['HMdb: Spencer State Hospital Cemetery marker','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=259445']],brick='?',conf='mixed',
  arch='Franzheim, Giesey and Faris; William Howe Patton',archw='SAH Archipedia: "Franzheim, Giesey and Faris of Wheeling, and William Howe Patton of Parkersburg" worked on its phases'),
N('lakin-state-hospital-wv','Lakin State Hospital for the Colored Insane','Lakin','wv','inst','marker',38.95403,-82.08705,'area','view','drive','','1926–',
  "The legislature created it in 1919 for Black West Virginians with mental illness, and it opened on February 1, 1926, with 162 patients moved from Weston. Wikipedia counts it one of only two known psychiatric hospitals run entirely by Black staff for Black patients; it was so poor it could not give every patient a sheet, and the lobotomist Walter Freeman came often. It was integrated in 1954, took over the Boys Industrial School in 1957, became Lakin Hospital in 1974 and ended adult psychiatric care in the late 1970s. In 2025 the governor announced its sale, with three other state long-term care hospitals, to a developer.",
  'A state marker of 2020. Most of the old buildings, among them brick dormitories, are gone; a building of the mid-1970s gives long-term care, and the state women\'s prison, Lakin Correctional Center, is next door.',
  '','The marker stands beside Ohio River Road (WV 62) at Bateman Circle, read from the road. The hospital and the prison are active state facilities: view from the road only.',
  [W('Lakin State Hospital'),['e-WV: Lakin State Hospital','https://wvencyclopedia.org/entries/1241'],['HMdb: Lakin State Hospital marker','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=315682']],brick='?'),
]
